9 = x^2 - 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}\n   \\]\n   Adding 2 to both sides, we get:\n   \\[\n   11 = x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2}\n   \\]\n\n4. Substitute \\( x^2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} = 11 \\) into the equation \\( y^2 = x^2 + 2 + \\frac{1}{x^2} \\):\n   \\[\n   y^2 = 11 + 2\n   \\]\n   Simplifying, we get:\n   \\[\n   y^2 = 13\n   \\]\n\n5. Since \\( y = x + \\frac{1}{x} \\) and \\( x \\) is a positive real number, \\( y \\) must be positive. Therefore:\n   \\[\n   y = \\sqrt{13}\n   \\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\sqrt{13}}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{13}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:751"}
